= Balancing Chemical Equations
*Grade 9 Chemistry Worksheet*

Name: #box(width: 8cm, line(length: 100%)) #h(1em) Date: #box(width: 3cm, line(length: 100%))

== Remember

- The Law of Conservation of Mass says atoms are not created or destroyed in a reaction, so each element must have the *same number of atoms* on both sides.
- Change only the *coefficients* (the big numbers in front). Never change the subscripts.
- Tip: balance elements that appear in only one compound first, and leave hydrogen and oxygen for last.
- If you write no number in a blank, it means 1, but please write the 1 anyway.

== Directions

Write a coefficient in each blank to balance the equation. The equations get harder as you go.

#let b = box(width: 1.5em, height: 1em, stroke: (bottom: 0.6pt))

=== Part A: Warm-up (1 to 4)

+ $ #b "H"_2 + #b "O"_2 -> #b "H"_2"O" $
+ $ #b "N"_2 + #b "H"_2 -> #b "NH"_3 $
+ $ #b "Mg" + #b "O"_2 -> #b "MgO" $
+ $ #b "Na" + #b "Cl"_2 -> #b "NaCl" $

=== Part B: Getting Tougher (5 to 8)

+ $ #b "CH"_4 + #b "O"_2 -> #b "CO"_2 + #b "H"_2"O" $
+ $ #b "Fe" + #b "O"_2 -> #b "Fe"_2"O"_3 $
+ $ #b "Al" + #b "HCl" -> #b "AlCl"_3 + #b "H"_2 $
+ $ #b "C"_3"H"_8 + #b "O"_2 -> #b "CO"_2 + #b "H"_2"O" $

=== Part C: Challenge (9 to 10)

+ $ #b "Al"_2 ("SO"_4)_3 + #b "NaOH" -> #b "Al" ("OH")_3 + #b "Na"_2 "SO"_4 $
+ $ #b "Ca" ("OH")_2 + #b "H"_3 "PO"_4 -> #b "Ca"_3 ("PO"_4)_2 + #b "H"_2"O" $

